{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"=== HANDOFF #22 — e369. READ THIS, THEN #21, THEN #20. ===\n\nWORKTREES: /Users/joeoakhart/rebar-jira-family (main working tree) · /Users/joeoakhart/agent-5200-diag\n(CLOSES, ff'd) · /Users/joeoakhart/wt-parentfix (branch fix-88d9-peer-evidence = change 1296) ·\n/Users/joeoakhart/wt-revert-88d9 (the merged revert). ALWAYS: export PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\".\n\n*** THE HEADLINE: A CHANGE I MERGED THIS SESSION DESTROYED DATA IN PRODUCTION. ***\n88d9's change 1288 (`6baa030460`) merged at 15:34 UTC. The scheduled bridge ran and between\n**15:37:59 and 15:38:10 UTC cleared the local `parent_id` of 63 TICKETS** across ~20 parents. The\noperator caught it (they saw 7 in the epic they were closing; the true radius was 63). It was the\n**PRODUCTION JIRA CLOUD bridge** (`reconcile-bridge.yml`, cron `*/20`) — so 88d9 was never a DC-only\nchange; it is shared core and Cloud is where it fired.\n\nMECHANISM — AND IT IS THE SAME WRONG PREMISE AS 2b16, WHICH I HAD PROVEN HOURS EARLIER.\n`diff_inbound_parent` cleared the local parent when the peer was OBSERVED parentless and the ref was in\n`managed_refs`. But `add_managed_ref` is folded by the parent-set EVENT, so a ref is \"managed\" the\ninstant it is set LOCALLY — managed NEVER proved the peer had it. **The shipped docstring said exactly\nthat**, then rested the whole feature on one compensating guard: same-pass suppression of an inbound\nfield outbound is writing. **That guard is structurally unreachable** — per 39c1 the outbound parent op\nis never emitted (emit gate skips non-epic parents, bug 8b25; DC's `set_parent` takes only sub-task\nchildren). So the \"residual window\" the docstring called degraded-pass-only was the NORMAL case.\nONE wrong premise about `managed_refs` produced TWO defects (2b16 and 88d9). On 2b16 an accidental\nsuppression kept the feature dormant and harmless; here the equivalent guard could never fire, so the\nsame premise became live data loss.\n\n--- WHAT I DID, IN ORDER (all verified, not assumed) ---\n 1. **Disabled `reconcile-bridge`** and cancelled the in-flight pass. It runs every 20 min, had already\n    run 5x on the bad code, and would have re-cleared anything repaired.\n 2. **Reverted 1288 whole** — change **1292**, now MERGED. Verified on the tree: `diff_inbound_parent`\n    is ABSENT from gerrit/main. **Bridge RE-ENABLED on that basis; it is active now.**\n 3. **Assessed the damage from the event log**: 63 reconciler-authored `EDIT {\"parent_id\":\"\"}` events,\n    all 63 prior parents recovered from CREATE/SNAPSHOT, all 63 confirmed CURRENTLY parentless (so\n    nothing newer could be overwritten), none previously de-parented by a human. Manifest:\n    `.claude/jobs/7604559b/tmp/repair.json`.\n 4. **Repair**: `rebar edit --parent` REFUSES a closed parent (`_commands/composer.py:503-513`) and 61 of\n    63 parents are closed. I checked ALL four candidate routes — no `parent`/`move`/`reparent` verb\n    exists, `edit` has NO `--force`, **`rebar revert` does NOT restore state** (`process_revert`\n    only appends to `state[\"reverts\"]`; the sole state effect is un-archiving), and `fsck-recover` is\n    git-level. **OPERATOR RULING: current state ACCEPTED for the closed tickets; the parent verb is an\n    IDEA, out of scope** — filed as `5992-f317-e05e-42f7`. The TWO non-closed victims fell outside that\n    acceptance and were repaired with a plain `edit --parent` (no force needed — their parents are still\n    open/in_progress): **437c-dd9d-9e65-46e7 -> 9594-1f1c-bcd1-4774, bf03-73bf-b66a-4c84 ->\n    4804-d9eb-e682-4d9c**, both verified on the reduced tickets.\n 5. **RED TEST FIRST, by operator direction — and it is RED against BOTH wrong states.**\n    `test_parent_clear_requires_peer_evidence.py`: the DEFECTIVE tree fails the negative cell (\"A local\n    parent rebar never pushed was cleared because it is in managed_refs\"), the REVERTED tree fails the\n    positive cell (\"the peer's last-synced parent was DC-9 and the peer now has NONE\"). Each wrong state\n    satisfies ONE half, so no single assertion separates correct from broken — which is precisely how\n    this repo keeps shipping oracles that cannot fail. A third cell holds the fail-open read safe.\n 6. **THE FIX: change 1296**, up for review. Adds the evidence `managed_refs` cannot supply —\n    `binding_store.get_peer_parent`/`set_peer_parent` (new binding-entry key beside the ADR-0026\n    baseline; ABSENT fails safe to NO CLEAR), advanced by `reconcile._advance_peer_parent` from\n    `_advance_baselines` **gated on `\"parent\" in entry`** (the load-bearing line: a fail-open read must\n    never overwrite a good observation), and `diff_inbound_parent` declining without it. `managed_refs`\n    stays as a further gate but NO LONGER DECIDES. 2079 passed / 1 xfailed across the FULL reconciler\n    unit + integration suites; lint, mypy, module-size clean. **Rollout: the first pass after it lands\n    clears NOTHING (no observations recorded yet) and self-heals as passes run.**\n\n--- OPERATOR RULINGS THIS SESSION (do not silently re-decide) ---\n * 2b16's certified plan must NOT be implemented as written — I proved its premise false BEFORE\n   starting (see #21). Recommendation on record: DEFER (it is a shared-core gap, identical on Cloud,\n   and it fails safe). **Still awaiting a decision.**\n * Dedicated `parent` verb with `--force`: IDEA, out of scope (`5992-f317-e05e-42f7`).\n * Store state ACCEPTED for the 61 closed victims; only non-closed ones repaired.\n * \"Fix the root cause, but start by adding a RED test\" -> done, steps 5-6 above.\n * `binding_store.py` headroom flagged by the operator: it went 749 -> 795, tightened to **787/800**.\n   Extraction of the last-synced-peer-state cluster filed as `4522-e600-9e2c-4823` rather than bundled\n   into a data-loss fix.\n\n--- NEXT ACTIONS ---\n 1. **Land 1296** (needs LLM-Review +1 + Verified +1). Then close 88d9 (`--class preexisting`).\n 2. **39c1 SHOULD BE RE-RULED.** It was filed non-gating/borderline. It is now the MECHANISM by which a\n    merged change destroyed 63 parent edges — no longer theoretical and demonstrably not fail-safe.\n 3. **A FRESH HARNESS RUN IS REQUIRED, and the one from this session is NOT usable.** Run\n    **30828166841** completed but ran against `6baa030460` — the DEFECTIVE main — so its\n    parent-clear verdicts are meaningless and main has since changed twice (revert + others). **I did\n    not read its verdicts; do not tick any 5200 AC off it.** Re-run after 1296 lands:\n    `gh workflow run external-integration.yml --repo navapbc/rebar --ref main`, job `jira-dc-harness`.\n 4. Then 5200 (18 ACs already verified; it waits only on a run), then e369 (**its plan review is\n   `stale-code` — re-run it LAST, immediately before the close**).\n 5. 2b16 still needs the operator's build-vs-defer call. If BUILD: the design is the SAME evidence\n    channel 1296 just introduced — a per-binding record of what the peer last had — so 1296 is the\n    template, and `binding_store` is already a parameter of both link differs.\n\n--- TRAPS PAID FOR THIS SESSION ---\n 1. **After a Gerrit submit, the GitHub mirror LAGS.** My first harness dispatch landed on the commit\n    BEFORE the merge and nothing in the output would have said so. `git fetch origin main` and confirm\n    your commit is on `origin/main` BEFORE dispatching a workflow on `main`.\n 2. **A docstring that documents a blind spot is not a mitigation.** 88d9's shipped docstring named the\n    exact flaw that destroyed the data and then leaned on a guard nobody checked was reachable. If a\n    fix's safety rests on another component firing, GO READ THAT COMPONENT.\n 3. `rebar revert` is an AUDIT record, not an undo. Verify before planning a repair around it.\n 4. The commit-msg hook rejects a body over 72 cols BEFORE committing (the file stays staged, nothing is\n    lost) — and `git commit --amend -F <file>` preserves the Change-Id only if the file CONTAINS the\n    Change-Id line. Extract it with `git log -1 --format=%B | grep Change-Id` first.\n 5. Running pytest in a worktree with no venv gives `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'rebar'` —\n    that is the missing venv on PATH, not a broken tree."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785774669276254001,"uuid":"f40c0ffa-1e64-44ba-aa04-d57b894d78df"}